---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const press = [
  {
    title: "The Love Machine Learning",
    outlet: "Towards Data Science",
    link: "https://medium.com/data-science/the-love-machine-learning-2cfa524a8c78",
  },
  {
    title: "The Politics of Romance",
    outlet: "The Startup (Medium)",
    link: "https://medium.com/swlh/the-politics-of-romance-fb41dbad5f19",
  },
  {
    title: "Machine Learning Fuels Campus Romance",
    outlet: "Georgia Tech College of Computing",
    link: "https://www.cc.gatech.edu/news/625853/machine-learning-fuels-campus-romance",
  },
  {
    title: "Algorithmic matchmaking: How a group of Georgia Tech students...",
    outlet: "11Alive",
    link: "https://www.11alive.com/video/news/education/algorithmic-matchmaking/85-0ed7e13b-ba6d-45af-8bea-777ff813778f",
  },
  {
    title: "Machine Learning in the Dating World",
    outlet: "Her Campus",
    link: "https://www.hercampus.com/school/ga-tech/machine-learning-dating-world/",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="The Love Machine · Case Study"
  description="Case study: an AI-powered campus matchmaking platform that paired 3,000+ students."
>
  <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">
    Case Study
  </p>
  <h1 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
    The Love Machine
  </h1>
  <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">Founder · AI + Web · December 2018 – March 2020</p>

  <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
    While in college, I founded and built an AI-powered matchmaking platform designed to help
    students meet compatible partners within the university community. The product used
    behavioral data and a genetic-algorithm approach to generate high-compatibility matches, and
    culminated in a campus-wide Valentine's Day match reveal and meetup event.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
    The platform scaled quickly, reaching 1,000 users in its first month and continuing to grow
    at a rate of 2x year-over-year.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-12 space-y-10">
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Problem</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        University campuses are dense social environments, yet many students struggle to meet
        compatible partners. Academic workloads, extracurricular commitments, and fragmented
        social circles limit opportunities for organic connections.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        At the same time, traditional dating apps introduce friction and safety concerns because
        users are interacting with strangers outside their social networks. Many potentially
        compatible students, therefore, never meet despite sharing the same campus community.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Insight</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        A university campus provides a dense, high-trust social network where algorithmic
        matchmaking can work particularly well. Students share physical proximity, social
        accountability, and overlapping communities, which makes them more comfortable meeting
        peers recommended by a compatibility system, even without prior interaction.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        If compatibility could be modeled using structured behavioral data, an algorithm could
        surface meaningful matches that might otherwise never occur.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Solution</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        I led the build of an AI-powered matchmaking platform that paired students based on
        personality traits, values, and relationship preferences. Participants completed a
        behavioral survey, which fed into a matching algorithm designed to optimize compatibility
        across the entire participant population.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">To create excitement and encourage real-world interaction:</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        <li>Matches were revealed simultaneously in a midnight "match drop" on Valentine's Day.</li>
        <li>Participants were invited to a Valentine's Day dinner event where they could meet their matches in person.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Key Product Decisions</h2>

      <h3 class="mt-5 font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        Designing for Participation: Behavioral Data Collection
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        To generate high-quality matches, the system required rich behavioral data. I designed a
        32-question survey capturing demographics, values, personality traits, lifestyle
        preferences, and relationship expectations.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        However, long surveys typically have low completion rates, especially among college
        students. To improve participation, I deliberately designed the survey to be humorous and
        easy to complete, using playful language, lighthearted response options, and Likert scales
        as opposed to text entry.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        This approach made the experience feel more like a campus social activity than a data
        form, which significantly increased engagement and encouraged students to share the
        survey with friends.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-6 font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        Building the Compatibility Scoring Model
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        I developed the scoring framework used to evaluate potential matches across the
        participant pool. The model combined:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        <li>Demographic constraints (e.g., gender preference, religion, political affiliation)</li>
        <li>Weighted personality similarity</li>
        <li>Squared distance scoring across Likert-scale responses to measure behavioral alignment</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        To improve match quality, I identified several relationship-critical attributes and
        applied weighted multipliers so those factors had greater influence in the matching
        algorithm. This scoring framework enabled the system to balance hard constraints (who can
        match) with soft compatibility signals (who should match best) before feeding candidate
        pairings into the optimization algorithm.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Launch Strategy</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        I modeled the release mechanic after streetwear drop culture: all matches revealed
        simultaneously at midnight on Valentine's Day, creating urgency, a shared campus moment,
        and immediate viral spread as students opened results together.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        At 12:00 AM on Valentine's Day, participants simultaneously received their algorithmically
        generated matches and could opt in to meet. The reveal was followed by a Valentine's Day
        dinner event, helping convert algorithmic matches into real-world interactions in a
        low-pressure environment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Growth &amp; Distribution</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">Initial user acquisition was driven through:</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        <li>Campus flyering</li>
        <li>Social media promotion</li>
        <li>Academic email lists</li>
        <li>Word-of-mouth referrals</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        Positioning the product as both a data-driven matchmaking experiment and a campus social
        event helped generate strong organic adoption.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="media-coverage" class="scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Media Coverage</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2">
        {press.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.link} class="text-neutral-700 underline hover:text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-300 dark:hover:text-neutral-100">
              {item.title}
            </a>
            <span class="text-sm text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400"> · {item.outlet}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-12 border-t border-neutral-200 pt-6 text-sm text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400 dark:border-neutral-800">
    <a href="/projects" class="underline hover:text-neutral-900 dark:hover:text-neutral-100">← Back to Projects</a>
  </p>
</Layout>
